# Shell and Python files must check out with LF, on every platform.
#
# Without this, core.autocrlf=true rewrites them to CRLF on a Windows checkout.
# The file still runs on Windows, but the moment the tree is copied to the Linux
# build host the kernel reads `#!/bin/bash\r` as the interpreter path and execve
# fails with ENOENT. The error names the script -- which is sitting right there,
# executable -- so it reads as "the script is missing" rather than "the shebang
# has a carriage return in it". That is what made `make unit` exit 127.
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.py   text eol=lf
